WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.791
It confidently invents an answer

00:00:03.091 --> 00:00:04.671
when the evidence is missing.

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:14.333
In twenty twenty-four, Air Canada's website chatbot told a grieving passenger he could claim a bereavement fare after he had travelled.

00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:17.748
The airline's own policy page said the opposite.

00:00:18.048 --> 00:00:26.390
Air Canada argued it wasn't responsible for what its chatbot said. The tribunal rejected that, and ordered the airline to pay.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.824
A fluent answer is not evidence.

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:35.714
QualityRag gives your application a third option between right and wrong: withhold.

00:00:36.014 --> 00:00:40.964
The rule fits in a few lines. Check first, and only call the model on admit.

00:00:41.264 --> 00:00:46.811
With one passage, the answer is withheld, and the code names exactly what's missing.

00:00:47.111 --> 00:00:51.187
Supply the evidence, and the same function lets the model answer.

00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:55.079
Here is a supported answer in the console.

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:58.025
Remove the memo that covers the exception.

00:00:58.325 --> 00:01:02.614
Not enough evidence. The answer is withheld, and the gap is named.

00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:05.397
The pattern is simple.

00:01:05.697 --> 00:01:09.665
Check first, answer only on admit, and show the gap otherwise.

00:01:09.965 --> 00:01:17.795
QualityRag won't search again by itself, and it can't stop a model you call without asking it. That part is your code.

00:01:18.095 --> 00:01:21.595
Next: three sources that agree, but aren't independent.
